WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.360
Right now, there are two completely

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:06.080
different housing markets happening in

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:08.320
America at the exact same time. In one

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.080
market, prices are crashing, homes

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.160
sitting for months. And in the other

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.559
market, prices are still climbing,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.720
inventory is bone dry, and buyers are

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:19.119
fighting over every listing. And the

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.560
fact is, most people have no idea which

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:22.560
market they're in. They're reading

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.119
national headlines that say either the

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.119
housing market is fine or the housing

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:29.119
market is crashing. and they're making

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:30.960
decisions based on information that

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.239
literally doesn't even apply to where

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.160
they live. Today, I'm going to break

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:35.760
down exactly what is happening, who the

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.559
winners and losers are going to be in

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.320
2026, and most importantly, what you

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:42.399
should actually do about it, depending

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.399
on your situation. The strategy that

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.000
worked for the last 5 years is not going

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:47.760
to work anymore. And the people who

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.600
understand that shift are going to make

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.360
a lot of money. And the ones who don't

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.440
are going to sit around wondering what

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.160
happened. The US housing market has

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.039
fractured into two separate realities.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.320
And this isn't speculative. JP Morgan,

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:04.320
Red Fin, Zillow, N are all confirming

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.640
the same thing. On one side, you have

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.119
the sunb belt. You have Florida, Texas,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.119
Arizona, Tennessee. These markets are

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:14.640
getting hammered. According to Zillow,

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:16.880
Texas home prices are down 2.4% from a

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.720
year ago. In Florida, home prices are

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.080
down 5.1%.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.320
As of December of 2025, 17 states,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.560
including major markets like Arizona,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.880
Florida, Texas, and Colorado, were

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.439
already back above 2019 inventory

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.960
levels. Zillow's data shows six straight

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:37.200
months of price drops in about 53% of US

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.040
cities. Now, flip to the Northeast and

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.759
the Midwest, and you have a completely

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.320
different story. Hartford, Rochester,

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.640
Buffalo, Providence, the inventory is

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.479
still tight. Prices are climbing three

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.799
to four percent because there simply

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:52.960
aren't enough homes and the jobs are

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.600
holding steady. These metros now

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.719
dominate Realtor.com's top market list

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.040
for 2026. A year ago though, the top 10

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:03.119
were mostly southern and Midwestern

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.520
cities. So when somebody asks you, "Is

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:08.080
the housing market crashing?" The honest

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.239
answer is which market? Because there is

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.400
no single housing market anymore. There

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:14.800
are winners and there are losers. And

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.720
which one you're in depends entirely on

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.200
your zip code. Now, here's where it gets

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.160
really interesting. On January 30th,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.120
Trump nominated Kevin Worsh to replace

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.360
Jerome Pal as the Fed chair. And this

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.840
matters more for housing than almost

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.680
anything else right now. Worsh former

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.319
Fed governor, the youngest ever

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.720
appointed. He served during the 2008

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:38.239
crisis. He's been openly critical about

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.239
money printing. And he's gone on the

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:43.040
record saying that when the Fed keeps

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:45.360
rates artificially low, the people who

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.080
benefit are the ones who already own

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.160
stocks and real estate, aka asset, not

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.239
the working family trying to make rent.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.480
And it is no secret that Trump wants

00:02:52.239 --> 00:02:57.120
lower rates. He's been calling Pal a

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.519
 for not cutting rates fast enough.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.680
But the guy he picked has historically

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:03.360
favored tighter money. That said, if you

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.760
pay close enough attention, it seems

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.080
like he's now in favor of easing in

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.319
2026, which would be very doubbish,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.800
driven by his belief that AI and

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:15.440
productivity gains can boost growth

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.280
without sparking inflation. So, he might

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.360
actually cut rates, but for different

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.200
reasons than people would expect. It

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.519
wasn't surprising that the markets

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.519
reacted to his nomination negatively.

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:27.760
Treasury rates climbed, the dollar

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:30.480
strengthened, gold dropped, stock

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.879
futures fell, all because Wall Street

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.879
expected a tighter Fed. The bottom line

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:37.599
is mortgage rates are probably not going

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:40.239
to go back to 3%. Every major forecaster

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:42.799
agrees that rates are staying above 6%

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:44.799
in 2026, and that rate environment is

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:47.040
creating a strangle hold on the market

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:49.200
from both sides of the equation. JP

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.040
Morgan's research shows over 80% of

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.519
mortgage holders are sitting on rates of

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.840
at least a full point below today's

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:58.239
market. They have zero incentive to sell

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.480
and reby at a higher rate. That's the

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:02.799
lockin effect and it's choking supply

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.720
from the inside. On top of that, hiring

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:07.280
has frozen thanks to things like

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.959
economic uncertainty and tariffs. People

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.640
aren't getting new job offers, which

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:12.560
means they're not relocating, which

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.239
means they're not listing their homes.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.079
So, two of the biggest engines that

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:18.560
normally drive housing activity are

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.560
stalled. This Fed transition creates a

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.560
level of unpredictability that we really

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.560
haven't seen in years. There's even a

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.960
political fight. Senator Tillis is

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.680
blocking Kevin Worsh's confirmation

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.919
until the DOJ investigation into PAL is

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.160
resolved. Nobody knows exactly what

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:36.000
happens next, and that's ultimately the

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.240
opportunity. So, with the market split

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.080
into two and a new Fed chair that nobody

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.160
can really predict, what are the actual

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.840
experts saying about where prices go

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.080
from here? Well, they can't even agree

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.759
with each other. JP Morgan is saying

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.160
flat 0% growth nationally in 2026. And

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:54.880
on the other hand, N says home prices

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.120
are forecasted to increase by 2%

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.479
nationally and existing home sales are

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.199
projected to rise by 14%. Meanwhile, Red

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:06.639
Fin projects a great housing reset in

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:09.600
2026 with US home sale prices rising by

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:11.199
only 1%. Nobody's predicting a crash,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.280
but depending on who you listen to,

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:15.759
you're either looking at a stall, a

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.680
reset, or a modest rebound. Now, here's

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:19.840
a contrarian take worth paying attention

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.759
to. You've heard that America is short 3

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.320
to 5 million homes. That number gets

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:26.400
thrown around constantly, but JP Morgan

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.800
challenges it. Their estimate is we're

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.560
only 1.2 2 million short, which is

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.720
significantly below other market

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.880
estimates. When they looked at 30 years

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.880
of data, household formations and

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.880
housing completions basically cancel

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.639
each other out. The structural gap is

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.560
actually a lot smaller than the industry

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.479
wants you to believe. Now, why does that

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.440
matter? Because if the shortage is more

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:49.840
modest than advertised, then the floor

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.759
under home prices is not as strong as

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.000
the bulls actually think. And builders

00:05:51.759 --> 00:05:56.639
have been stacking up unsold inventory.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.320
Right now, there are 481,000 new homes

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:01.520
on the market, which is the highest

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.319
level since 2008. Similarly, spec homes

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.639
for sale are at 385,000, which is the

00:06:04.319 --> 00:06:08.560
highest since '08. These are pre-crash

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:10.720
level numbers on the new construction

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.120
side. Meanwhile, affordability is

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.360
improving for the first time in a really

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.520
long time. Monthly payments are expected

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.840
to decline for the first time since

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.759
2020. Existing home sales hit a

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.800
three-year high in December, which is up

00:06:21.759 --> 00:06:28.000
5.1%. Mortgage purchase applications saw

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:31.280
a major spike in early November of 25.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.600
The MBA reported a 31% jump compared to

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.400
the same week the prior year, the

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.120
strongest November start since 2022.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.039
There are 4.8 million borrowers who

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.840
could benefit from refinancing. Right

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:47.039
now, N estimates that a onepoint drop in

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.960
rates unlocks roughly 5.5 million

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:51.600
additional qualifying households,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.080
including 1.6 million renters who have

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.240
never owned. Even if one in 10 of those

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.639
people buy, that's nearly a half a

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.880
million new transactions. So, the demand

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:02.960
exists, but in the Sunb Belt, rate cuts

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.280
alone are not fixing it. Despite

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.440
multiple Fed cuts, the demand keeps

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.599
dropping in those markets. Prices need

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.960
to come down, not just rates, and that's

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:12.400
exactly what's happening. Here's

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.720
something that nobody's connecting the

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.400
dots on. Trump has proposed banning

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.479
institutional investors from buying

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.479
single family homes. Sounds great on

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:22.400
paper, right? Except JP Morgan points

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:25.039
out that institutional buyers only

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.800
account for 1 to 3% of purchases, which

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:28.479
is barely a blip. And many of those

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.400
investors have pivoted to building

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:32.400
rental communities. So sure, as much as

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.319
it would be satisfying to blame Black

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.319
Rockck and other big corporations, if

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:38.479
you ban them from buying, you might

00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:40.639
accidentally stop them from building,

00:07:38.479 --> 00:07:42.479
which tightens supply even more and

00:07:40.639 --> 00:07:44.720
makes affordability worse. The cure

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:46.000
could be worse than the disease. And

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.840
here's something that should really make

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.560
you pay attention. At a cabinet meeting

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:53.360
on January 29th, Trump said he doesn't

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.759
want to drive housing prices down. He

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.400
wants to drive them up for people who

00:07:55.759 --> 00:08:00.560
already own homes. The president is

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.039
openly saying that his goal is to

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.199
protect existing homeowner equity, not

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:07.120
to make homes cheaper for firsttime home

00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:09.199
buyers. And that tells you everything

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.120
about where policy is headed. So, you've

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:13.680
got a new Fed chair who's historically

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.639
hawkish, a president who wants prices to

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.960
go up, policy proposals that JP Morgan

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:20.960
says won't move the needle, and a market

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.879
that's splitting in half geographically.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.560
That's the landscape. Now, what do you

00:08:22.879 --> 00:08:26.960
do about it? If you're in a sunb belt

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.960
market, places like Florida, Texas,

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.360
Arizona, Tennessee, then you're in the

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.919
driver's seat as a buyer. Inventory is

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.560
up, prices are falling, sellers are more

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:38.959
flexible. So, don't rush. Negotiate

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.360
aggressively. The worst that they say is

00:08:38.959 --> 00:08:43.120
no. And in these markets, they might say

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.000
yes because their homes probably been

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.240
sitting on the market for 60 to 120

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.160
days, maybe even longer. Some analysts

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.240
expect Florida's slide to continue

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.920
through the first half of 2026 before

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.000
kind of flattening out. If you're a

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.839
seller here, price realistically. Homes

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.680
that are priced right are still moving,

00:08:57.839 --> 00:09:02.240
but homes priced based on what your

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.920
neighbor got in 2021 or 2022 are

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.600
sitting. If you're in the Northeast or

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.240
the Midwest, different playbook

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.240
entirely. Inventory is tight, prices are

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.080
rising, and there's no crash coming in

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.760
these markets. If you're ready, move

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.519
sooner rather than later before this

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.360
spring buying season brings more

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:18.959
competition. And another tip, pay

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.880
attention to what builders are doing.

00:09:18.959 --> 00:09:22.640
They're desperate to move inventory, and

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.399
they are essentially paying to lower

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.120
your mortgage rate upfront. We're

00:09:24.399 --> 00:09:29.120
talking one to two full points off

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.839
market rate. While the 30-year fixed

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.839
sits above 6%, you could walk into a new

00:09:31.839 --> 00:09:36.000
build and lock something in the low to

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:37.839
mid4s. I've flipped hundreds of homes

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.920
and wholesaliled thousands of properties

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:43.040
across the US. And the best deals that

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.519
I've ever done were during times exactly

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.680
like this when everybody's confused, the

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:49.200
headlines are contradictory, and people

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.800
are arguing about whether it's a good

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.399
time to buy or not. That's when the

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.800
prepared buyer always wins. 2026 is not

00:09:54.399 --> 00:09:59.200
2008 in my opinion. Lending standards

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.200
are way too tight. Homeowners have so

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.040
much equity and they're not being forced

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.279
to sell. There's still a national

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.040
housing shortfall. The question isn't

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:09.040
whether there's opportunity. There

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:11.360
always is opportunity. The question is

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.680
whether you're positioned to capture it.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.399
Get your debt down. Get preapproved.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.240
Know your numbers and study your local

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:20.240
market. Because when that window of

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.959
opportunity opens, the people who are

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.360
ready will move and everybody else will

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:27.279
watch from the sidelines. If I made this

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.120
housing market mess all make sense for

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:31.120
you, do me a huge favor. Hit that

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.360
subscribe. Drop a comment what market

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.279
you're in and what you see going on in

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.040
your local market. Are you buying? Are

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:38.320
you selling? Or are you waiting? Thank

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.000
you so much for staying to the very end

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:42.240
and I look forward to seeing you in the

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.240
next

